WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.250
My robot needed sixteen cards to walk

2
00:00:03.250 --> 00:00:05.630
round the playground square: forward,

3
00:00:06.090 --> 00:00:09.910
forward, forward, turn, again and again.

4
00:00:10.510 --> 00:00:13.310
Then a pupil said: Miss, it’s

5
00:00:13.310 --> 00:00:15.110
the same thing four times!

6
00:00:15.760 --> 00:00:17.360
She had just discovered loops.

7
00:00:18.810 --> 00:00:20.950
Today: loops, and how they

8
00:00:20.950 --> 00:00:22.150
make programs shorter.

9
00:00:23.450 --> 00:00:24.850
Look at the robot’s program.

10
00:00:25.630 --> 00:00:28.490
Forward three times, then turn right.

11
00:00:29.150 --> 00:00:32.390
Then the same again, and again, and again.

12
00:00:33.070 --> 00:00:35.710
When steps repeat, there is a pattern,

13
00:00:36.350 --> 00:00:38.290
and a pattern can become a loop.

14
00:00:39.530 --> 00:00:42.390
A loop repeats a block of steps a number

15
00:00:42.390 --> 00:00:45.130
of times, or until something happens.

16
00:00:45.890 --> 00:00:48.290
Instead of sixteen cards, you write:

17
00:00:48.590 --> 00:00:51.770
repeat four times, forward three, turn

18
00:00:51.770 --> 00:00:52.010
right.

19
00:00:52.670 --> 00:00:54.950
Four cards do the work of sixteen.

20
00:00:56.500 --> 00:00:58.920
Loops make programs shorter, but

21
00:00:58.920 --> 00:01:00.600
that is not the best part.

22
00:01:01.250 --> 00:01:02.230
Want a bigger square?

23
00:01:02.960 --> 00:01:06.140
Change one number, not sixteen cards.

24
00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:09.020
Fewer cards means fewer

25
00:01:09.020 --> 00:01:10.680
places for a bug to hide.

26
00:01:11.920 --> 00:01:13.760
Children already know loops.

27
00:01:14.570 --> 00:01:16.370
A song’s chorus comes back

28
00:01:16.370 --> 00:01:17.450
after every verse.

29
00:01:18.180 --> 00:01:20.460
A clapping game repeats the same pattern.

30
00:01:21.110 --> 00:01:23.530
A dance says: step, step,

31
00:01:23.990 --> 00:01:25.610
clap, four times.

32
00:01:26.250 --> 00:01:28.730
Writing it once and saying how many

33
00:01:28.730 --> 00:01:31.290
times is exactly what a loop does.

34
00:01:32.610 --> 00:01:33.190
Quick check!

35
00:01:33.980 --> 00:01:38.000
My program is six forward cards in a row.

36
00:01:38.630 --> 00:01:42.630
If one card can repeat up to four times,

37
00:01:43.250 --> 00:01:46.310
how few cards can do the same walk?

38
00:01:47.600 --> 00:01:49.640
Two cards: forward four

39
00:01:49.640 --> 00:01:51.820
times, then forward twice.

40
00:01:52.470 --> 00:01:54.770
One card can’t do six, because a

41
00:01:54.770 --> 00:01:57.190
card repeats at most four times.

42
00:01:57.880 --> 00:01:59.660
Limits like this make pupils

43
00:01:59.660 --> 00:02:01.560
think, and that’s the point.

44
00:02:02.880 --> 00:02:05.380
Later, the same idea becomes code.

45
00:02:06.090 --> 00:02:08.410
In Python, a loop starts with

46
00:02:08.410 --> 00:02:10.890
for, and the lines inside it are

47
00:02:10.890 --> 00:02:11.950
pushed in from the edge.

48
00:02:12.580 --> 00:02:14.280
This one walks the whole square.

49
00:02:15.050 --> 00:02:16.750
The robot cards and the code

50
00:02:16.750 --> 00:02:17.970
are the same thinking.

51
00:02:19.260 --> 00:02:21.240
Before running a loop, ask

52
00:02:21.240 --> 00:02:22.340
pupils to predict.

53
00:02:23.160 --> 00:02:25.200
How many squares will the robot walk?

54
00:02:25.880 --> 00:02:27.620
Where will it stop, and

55
00:02:27.620 --> 00:02:28.840
which way will it face?

56
00:02:29.630 --> 00:02:31.530
Predicting first turns

57
00:02:31.530 --> 00:02:32.870
guessing into thinking.

58
00:02:34.140 --> 00:02:36.740
In the app, open Program a robot

59
00:02:36.740 --> 00:02:38.800
and try grids four to six.

60
00:02:39.550 --> 00:02:41.810
Tap a card in your program to repeat it.

61
00:02:42.370 --> 00:02:44.650
Then go for three stars: the

62
00:02:44.650 --> 00:02:46.090
fewest cards possible.

63
00:02:47.370 --> 00:02:47.910
Your turn.

64
00:02:48.520 --> 00:02:51.320
My robot runs: repeat three

65
00:02:51.320 --> 00:02:53.340
times, forward two.

66
00:02:53.930 --> 00:02:55.510
How many squares does it move?

67
00:02:56.760 --> 00:02:57.100
Six.

68
00:02:57.800 --> 00:03:00.400
Remember: repeated steps are a pattern.

69
00:03:01.030 --> 00:03:04.030
A loop repeats a block a number of times.

70
00:03:04.660 --> 00:03:06.100
It makes programs shorter

71
00:03:06.100 --> 00:03:07.520
and easier to change.

72
00:03:08.260 --> 00:03:10.520
And pupils already know loops

73
00:03:10.520 --> 00:03:12.000
from songs and games.

74
00:03:13.390 --> 00:03:15.790
Try it in class tomorrow: dance loops.

75
00:03:16.530 --> 00:03:19.810
Groups invent a short dance, write it as a

76
00:03:19.810 --> 00:03:22.750
program with repeat, and swap programs.

77
00:03:23.550 --> 00:03:25.010
Can another group perform it

78
00:03:25.010 --> 00:03:26.410
exactly from the paper?
